=== layout: section

# Animal BioTech

=== layout: subsection

# 12. Artificial Life

=== layout: bookend

# 12. Questions

@body

- What makes a system qualify as artificial life?
- How do artificial life simulations help scientists study evolution, complexity, and self-organization?

=== layout: one-panel

# 12. Test a Definition of Life

@body

- A computer model can represent variation, inheritance, and selection.
- A model produces outcomes from rules chosen by its designers.
- A biological cell also carries out physical and chemical processes such as metabolism.

@citation

[Lenski et al., The evolutionary origin of complex features (2003)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12736677/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 12. Which Evidence Would Change Your Answer?

@left

*Digital population*

Programs copy themselves. Their descendants evolve new behaviors.

@right

*Chemical compartment*

Reactions continue inside a membrane. The compartment cannot yet reproduce. Which properties belong in your definition of life?

@citation

[Kriegman et al., Reconfigurable living organisms (2020)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31932426/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 12. Xenobots and the Definition of Artificial Life

@left

![Michael Levin, Xenobot coauthor](assets/set7_people_animals/michael_levin.jpeg)

@right

Michael Levin was one of the Xenobot researchers. The team used living frog cells to make small structures that moved in a dish.

This case sits between a computer simulation and an ordinary animal. Which properties came from the cells already being alive?

@citation

[Photo: Tufts Biology, Michael Levin](https://as.tufts.edu/biology/people/faculty/michael-levin)  |  [Kriegman et al., Reconfigurable living organisms (2020)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31932426/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 12. What Can Evolve Inside a Computer?

@left

![Richard Lenski, digital-evolution researcher](assets/set7_people_animals/richard_lenski.png)

@right

Richard Lenski and colleagues studied programs that copied themselves. Inherited changes sometimes altered what descendants could compute.

An environment rewarded some computations, so lineages left different numbers of descendants. What does that establish about evolution? What does it leave open about life?

@citation

[Photo: American Society for Microbiology, Richard Lenski](https://asm.org/biographies/richard-lenski-2020-dc-white-award)  |  [Lenski et al., The evolutionary origin of complex features (2003)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12736677/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 12. What was alive at the start?

@left

![Adult African clawed frog](assets/set7_photos/xenopus_adult.webp)

@right

This is the African clawed frog, *Xenopus laevis*. The cells in the Xenobot study came from frog embryos.

The cells were already alive. What did the researchers design?

@citation

[Photo: Brian Gratwicke, CC BY 2.0](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xenopus_laevis_01.jpg)  |  [Kriegman et al. (2020)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31932426/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 12. Living Cells, Designed Shape

@left

![Josh Bongard, Xenobot coauthor](assets/set7_people_animals/josh_bongard.jpg)

@right

Josh Bongard helped design the Xenobots. A computer suggested shapes. Living frog cells formed the structures.

Which part of the project is a design decision, and which part comes from the properties of living cells?

@citation

[Photo: University of Vermont, Josh Bongard](https://www.uvm.edu/cems/cs/profile/josh-bongard)  |  [Kriegman et al., Reconfigurable living organisms (2020)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31932426/)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Results from 50 independent populations of digital organisms](assets/set7_animals_evidence/avida_outcomes.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 12: Same rewards, different results\
In 23 of 50 populations, programs evolved a way to test whether two bits match.

@citation

[Lenski et al., The evolutionary origin of complex features (2003)](https://www.nature.com/articles/nature01568)

@speaker-notes

The software rewarded simpler operations too. The other 27 populations did not evolve this particular operation during the experiment. They could still evolve other abilities.

=== layout: subsection

# 13. Xenobiology and Non-DNA Life

=== layout: bookend

# 13. Questions

@body

- How do alternative information molecules challenge our definition of a gene?
- Could xenobiology help answer questions about possible alien life?

=== layout: one-panel

# 13. An Information Molecule Is a Physical Object

@body

- On Earth, DNA and RNA carry genetic information in chains of nucleotides.
- Xeno nucleic acids are synthetic analogues that change some of that chemistry.
- Storing information is one property. A living system also needs to read, copy, and act on information.

@citation

[Pinheiro et al., Synthetic genetic polymers (2012)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22517858/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 13. Where Does the Claim Stop?

@body

- Evidence that a molecule carries a sequence establishes a molecular property.
- A claim about an independently living organism would require organism-level evidence.
- What observation would distinguish those two claims?

@citation

[Pinheiro et al., Synthetic genetic polymers (2012)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22517858/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 13. What Does the X in XNA Change?

@body

Imagine two chains that carry the same order of bases. In one, the chemical backbone differs from the one in DNA.

- Sequence is the order of the symbols. The backbone is the material that holds them in order.
- Can you tell which material a message was written on by reading only the message?

@citation

[Pinheiro et al., Synthetic genetic polymers (2012)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22517858/)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Read Both Chains](assets/set7_drawings/xna_backbones.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 13: Read Both Chains\
The letters match at every position. Which feature of the molecules changed?

@citation

[Pinheiro et al., Synthetic genetic polymers (2012)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22517858/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 13. What Did the XNA Experiment Establish?

@body

The researchers showed that information could be copied between DNA and several synthetic polymers. Some XNA populations also responded to selection.

- That is evidence that heredity can use different molecular backbones.
- The experiment depended on a laboratory system. An organism living independently would be a much larger claim.

@citation

[Pinheiro et al., Synthetic genetic polymers (2012)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22517858/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 13. Same Sequence, Different Chemistry

@left

*What was tested*

- Six synthetic genetic polymers
- Information transfer between DNA and XNA
- Selection of XNA molecules in vitro

@right

*What remains to show*

- A self-maintaining cell
- Independent reproduction
- An ecosystem using the chemistry

@citation

[Pinheiro et al., Synthetic genetic polymers (2012)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22517858/)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![DNA and XNA Evidence Scope](assets/set7_animals_evidence/xna_scope.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 13: Can the same message travel through XNA?\
Trace the base sequence from DNA to XNA and back again.

@citation

[Pinheiro et al., Synthetic genetic polymers (2012)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22517858/)

=== layout: subsection

# 14. Xenobiology and Non-Organic Life

=== layout: bookend

# 14. Questions

@body

- What properties make carbon useful in living systems on Earth?
- What would be the challenges of identifying alien life if its chemistry does not match known Earth molecules?

=== layout: one-panel

# 14. Backbone and Solvent Are Different Roles

@body

- Carbon forms varied, stable structures under conditions found on Earth.
- Water provides a medium for chemical reactions in Earth life.
- Titan's methane and ethane lakes motivate questions about alternative solvents. Other biochemistries remain hypotheses.

@citation

[NASA, Titan facts](https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/titan/facts/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 14. A Signal Can Have More Than One Source

@body

- Suppose an extraterrestrial sample contains unexpected molecules.
- Ask whether known geology, atmospheric chemistry, or contamination can explain them.
- Look for independent evidence before calling the signal a sign of life.

@citation

[NASA, Ladder of Life Detection](https://science.nasa.gov/astrobiology/researchers/life-detection-resources/ladder-of-life-detection/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 14. Does a Methane Lake Contain Organic Molecules?

@body

Yes. Methane and ethane both contain carbon. On Titan they can be liquids at the surface.

- A different solvent and a different element for building large molecules are two separate ideas.
- The lakes are observed. Life using those lakes remains hypothetical.

@citation

[NASA, Titan facts](https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/titan/facts/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 14. What Would Convince a Second Lab?

@body

Imagine a sample contains a molecule that living organisms make on Earth. A nonliving chemical reaction could make it too.

- Check how the sample was collected and whether the signal also appears in a blank.
- A second, independent sign of biological activity would change the weight of the evidence.

@citation

[NASA, Ladder of Life Detection](https://science.nasa.gov/astrobiology/researchers/life-detection-resources/ladder-of-life-detection/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 14. What did Viking find on Mars?

@left

You can see Viking 1's footpad beside the rocks. Viking's instruments also detected chemical activity in soil samples.

Living cells could produce a signal. So could a chemical reaction in the soil. What evidence would let you choose?

@right

![Lander footpad on the rocky Martian surface](assets/set7_case_photos/viking_footpad.webp)

@citation

[NASA/JPL, Viking 1 image](https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA10738)  |  [NASA, Viking project](https://science.nasa.gov/astrobiology/missions/viking-project/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 14. A Life-Detection Claim Needs a Ladder

@left

*One observation*

An unexpected molecule appears in a sample.

- It might have a nonliving source.
- It might be contamination.

@right

*A stronger case*

- Independent measurements agree
- Nonliving explanations are tested
- The result is reproduced

@citation

[NASA, Ladder of Life Detection](https://science.nasa.gov/astrobiology/researchers/life-detection-resources/ladder-of-life-detection/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 14. Can you find a shoreline on Titan?

@left

![Cassini radar image of lake shores on Titan](assets/set7_case_photos/titan_lakes.webp)

@right

Cassini sent a radar pulse toward Titan. The smooth lake surface reflects little of that signal back to the spacecraft. Trace the dark shoreline.

What evidence would it take to go from a lake of hydrocarbons to a claim about life?

@citation

[NASA/JPL, Cassini radar, October 2006](https://science.nasa.gov/resource/lakes-and-more-lakes/)  |  [NASA, Titan facts](https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/moons/titan/facts/)

=== layout: subsection

# 15. Cloning Animals

=== layout: bookend

# 15. Questions

@body

- How does the process of animal cloning technically work?
- What are the success rates and potential risks associated with animal cloning?
- What services are available for cloning animals? Should people be allowed to clone their pets? How about cloning a race horse? Cloning a dead relative? Who is Hwang Woo-suk and what happened in his lab? Are we able to modify clones, such as change their eye color or sex?

=== layout: two-panels

# 15. Where did Dolly get her DNA?

@left

This is Dolly at the National Museum of Scotland. Her nuclear DNA came from an adult sheep's mammary cell.

Another sheep supplied the egg. Which DNA did Dolly inherit from her?

@right

![Dolly on display at the National Museum of Scotland](assets/set7_photos/dolly_museum.webp)

@citation

[Photo: Sgerbic, CC BY-SA 4.0](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dolly_the_Sheep_National_Museum_of_Scotland.jpg)  |  [Evans et al., mitochondrial DNA in cloned sheep](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10471506/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 15. Would a clone behave like your dog?

@left

![Ian Wilmut with Dolly](assets/set7_people_animals/ian_wilmut_dolly.jpg)

@right

Ian Wilmut is pictured with Dolly, the sheep his team cloned. A clone starts with similar nuclear DNA. Its experiences and behavior can differ.

What would a company need to disclose about egg donors, pregnancies, and offspring before taking a customer's money?

@citation

[Photo: Science Photo Library via ABPI Schools](https://www.abpischools.org.uk/topics/biotechnology/biotechnology-timeline/)  |  [NHGRI, Cloning fact sheet](https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/fact-sheets/Cloning-Fact-Sheet)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Nuclear Transfer in Animal Cloning](assets/set7_instructor/animal_cloning.jpg)

@caption

Topic 15: Nuclear Transfer in Animal Cloning\
Trace the donor nucleus, egg, embryo, and surrogate in the pig-cloning diagram.

@citation

Course image: instructor ethics deck, p. 36.

=== layout: one-panel

# 15. Whose DNA Would a Test Find?

@body

A sheep provides a body cell, a second provides the egg, and a third carries the pregnancy.

- A nuclear marker can link the lamb to the body-cell donor.
- In the cloned sheep studied by Evans and colleagues, mitochondrial DNA matched the egg source. Carrying the pregnancy is a third role.

@citation

[Evans et al., Mitochondrial DNA in cloned sheep (1999)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10471506/)  |  [Wilmut et al., Viable offspring from adult cells (1997)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9039911/)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Trace Two Genetic Contributions](assets/set7_drawings/clone_dna.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 15: Two DNA Sources\
Which donor supplied each kind of DNA in these cloned sheep?

@citation

[Evans et al., Mitochondrial DNA in cloned sheep (1999)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10471506/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 15. What Is the Denominator for Cloning Success?

@body

Imagine a report of two healthy births from ten pregnancies after 50 embryo transfers.

- Per pregnancy, that is 2 out of 10. Per embryo transferred, it is 2 out of 50.
- Both fractions are correct for this invented example. Which one would you want when assessing animal welfare?

@citation

[NHGRI, Cloning fact sheet](https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/fact-sheets/Cloning-Fact-Sheet)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Dolly study counts for embryos, recipient ewes and live lambs](assets/set7_animals_evidence/dolly_funnel.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 15: One lamb. Which denominator?\
1 of 29 transferred embryos (3.4%) or 1 of 277 reconstructed embryos (0.36%).

@citation

[Wilmut et al., Viable offspring from adult cells (1997)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9039911/)

@speaker-notes

For the adult mammary-cell group, 29 embryos went to 13 recipients. The two percentages answer different questions. Neither is a general success rate for animal cloning.

=== layout: two-panels

# 15. Three Animals, Three Roles

@left

*Body-cell donor*

Provides nuclear DNA.

*Egg donor*

Provides egg cytoplasm and mitochondrial DNA.

@right

*Surrogate*

Carries the pregnancy.

*Question*

Which role is missing if an ad says only "your pet's DNA"?

@citation

[Evans et al., Mitochondrial DNA in cloned sheep (1999)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10471506/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 15. Similar Genome Does Not Mean Same Dog

@body

- A clone begins with nuclear DNA like the donor's.
- The egg, pregnancy, development, training, and life history also matter.
- "Bring back your dog" is a much bigger claim than "produce a genetically similar puppy."

@citation

[NHGRI, Cloning fact sheet](https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/fact-sheets/Cloning-Fact-Sheet)

=== layout: subsection

# 16. Animal De-Extinction

=== layout: bookend

# 16. Questions

@body

- What specific technologies are used in the de-extinction of animals? How does the process work?
- What endpoint did the Pyrenean ibex cloning study reach?
- What are the limitations of current technology in reviving extinct species? What is the half-life of DNA? Which animals are we able to bring back? dinosaurs? mammoths? dodos? extinct plants?
- How could de-extinction impact biodiversity conservation efforts?

=== layout: one-panel

# 16. The DNA and Habitat Problems

@body

- Ancient DNA breaks into fragments. The environment affects how long those fragments survive.
- A proxy made using a living relative can share selected traits with an extinct species.
- The habitat, other species, and learned behavior may also have changed.

@citation

[Allentoft et al., DNA decay in dated bones (2012)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23055061/)  |  [IUCN, Proxies of extinct species (2016)](https://iucn.org/resources/grey-literature/iucn-ssc-guiding-principles-creating-proxies-extinct-species-conservation)

=== layout: one-panel

# 16. What Counts as Success?

@body

- For a mammoth-like proxy, choose a measure: appearance, cold tolerance, or a role in the ecosystem.
- Compare the cost and welfare concerns with conservation of living species.
- Would the project still count as a success if the animal could only live in captivity?

@citation

[IUCN, Proxies of extinct species (2016)](https://iucn.org/resources/grey-literature/iucn-ssc-guiding-principles-creating-proxies-extinct-species-conservation)

=== layout: one-panel

# 16. The Pyrenean Ibex

@body

- A cloned Pyrenean ibex was born alive and died soon after birth.
- A live birth and a healthy population are very different endpoints.
- For a mammoth proxy, habitat and welfare would also enter the decision.

@citation

[Folch et al., Cloning the Pyrenean ibex (2009)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19167744/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 16. Does All DNA Have a 521-year Half-life?

@body

Researchers studied 158 dated moa bones from sites in New Zealand. For one 242-base-pair mitochondrial DNA fragment, they estimated a half-life of 521 years.

- That estimate describes a particular fragment and burial environment.
- Finding a short sequence in a bone does not mean an intact donor nucleus survived.

@citation

[Allentoft et al., DNA decay in dated bones (2012)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23055061/)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Apply the Bone Study Estimate Twice](assets/set7_drawings/fragment_decay.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 16: Apply the Bone Study Estimate Twice\
If half the intact fragments remain after one half-life, what fraction remains after two?

@citation

[Allentoft et al., DNA decay in dated bones (2012)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23055061/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 16. A Birth, a Population, a Habitat

@body

The ibex team began with cells preserved while the animal was alive. The cloned newborn died within minutes from breathing problems.

- One birth establishes that a particular developmental endpoint was reached.
- A conservation project also needs animals that survive, reproduce, and can live in a suitable habitat.

@citation

[Folch et al., Cloning the Pyrenean ibex (2009)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19167744/)  |  [IUCN, Proxies of extinct species (2016)](https://iucn.org/resources/grey-literature/iucn-ssc-guiding-principles-creating-proxies-extinct-species-conservation)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Outcomes in the Pyrenean ibex cloning study](assets/set7_animals_evidence/ibex_endpoints.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 16: Did the Pyrenean ibex come back?\
One kid was born alive. How far did the project get toward a breeding population?

@citation

[Folch et al., Cloning the Pyrenean ibex (2009)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19167744/)

@speaker-notes

A live birth matters to a developmental biologist. To sustain a population the animals would also have to reach reproductive age, produce offspring and have suitable habitat.

=== layout: two-panels

# 16. The Ibex Result Had a Narrow Endpoint

@left

*What happened*

- One cloned Pyrenean ibex was born alive
- It died soon after birth from respiratory problems

@right

*What did not follow*

- A viable breeding population
- Restored ecological interactions
- A general method for any extinct animal

@citation

[Folch et al., Cloning the Pyrenean ibex (2009)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19167744/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 16. A Proxy Is a Conservation Choice

@body

IUCN describes a proxy as an organism created to resemble an extinct species for conservation purposes.

- The word keeps an important distinction visible. A proxy has selected similarities. It is not the original species.
- Any proposed release still needs a habitat, a conservation goal, and a welfare plan.

@citation

[IUCN, Proxies of extinct species (2016)](https://iucn.org/resources/grey-literature/iucn-ssc-guiding-principles-creating-proxies-extinct-species-conservation)

=== layout: subsection

# 17. Animal Chimeras

=== layout: bookend

# 17. Questions

@body

- What is an animal chimera, and how does it differ from a hybrid created by crossing two species?
- What methods are used to create chimeras between different animals, and what scientific goals do these studies support?
- Which real examples of animal chimeras have been made in research, and what traits did they show?
- What ethical concerns arise when mixing tissues or organs from two different animal species?
- How might chimeras be used in agriculture, conservation, or disease research, and what limits should be set on these uses?

=== layout: one-panel

# 17. Separate Cell Populations or One Cross?

@body

{table-style=standard}
| Term | Where did the different DNA come from? |
| --- | --- |
| Chimera | Genetically distinct cell groups came from different fertilized eggs. |
| Hybrid | Parents from distinct species or lines contributed DNA at fertilization. |
| Mosaic | Genetically distinct cell groups arose from the same fertilized egg. |

@citation

[NHGRI, Mosaicism](https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Mosaicism)  |  [ISSCR, Guidelines for stem cell research and clinical translation](https://www.isscr.org/resources/guidelines-for-stem-cell-research)

=== layout: one-panel

# 17. Where Do the Cells Contribute?

@body

- Researchers may track donor cells to understand development or model disease.
- The ethical questions change with the tissue involved, the proportion of donor cells, and effects on the animal.
- An oversight committee can ask how the investigators will determine where donor cells went.

@citation

[ISSCR, Guidelines for stem cell research and clinical translation](https://www.isscr.org/resources/guidelines-for-stem-cell-research)

=== layout: one-panel

# 17. When Did the Difference Appear?

@body

Start with one fertilized egg. A DNA change occurs when one of its descendant cells divides. Only that branch of the family tree carries it.

- This gives one individual two cell populations with different genotypes.
- Sampling only the unaffected tissue could miss the change.

@citation

[NHGRI, Mosaicism](https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Mosaicism)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Trace Each Cell Group Back](assets/set7_drawings/cell_lineages.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 17: Trace the Cell Groups\
A and B are different genotypes. Where did the difference arise?

@citation

[NHGRI, Mosaicism](https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Mosaicism)  |  [ISSCR, Guidelines for stem cell research and clinical translation](https://www.isscr.org/resources/guidelines-for-stem-cell-research)

=== layout: one-panel

# 17. Two People Contribute to One Blood Sample

@body

After a successful donor marrow transplant, blood-forming cells from the donor can make new blood cells in the recipient.

- A blood sample can therefore carry donor DNA. Many cells in other tissues still carry the recipient's DNA.
- What would you need to know before interpreting a DNA test in this person?

@citation

[MedlinePlus, Bone marrow transplant](https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/003009.htm)  |  [ISSCR, Guidelines for stem cell research and clinical translation](https://www.isscr.org/resources/guidelines-for-stem-cell-research)

=== layout: two-panels

# 17. Chimera, Hybrid, and Mosaic Answer Different Questions

@left

*Chimera*

Cell populations trace to different embryos.

*Hybrid*

Parents from different species or distinct genetic lines produce offspring. Their nuclear DNA comes together at fertilization.

@right

*Mosaic*

One embryo develops a second cell population after a genetic change.

*Check*

Ask when the distinct DNA first came together.

@citation

[NHGRI, Mosaicism](https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Mosaicism)  |  [ISSCR, Guidelines](https://www.isscr.org/resources/guidelines-for-stem-cell-research)

=== layout: one-panel

# 17. Which DNA is in the tube?

@body

A patient received bone marrow from a donor. A lab can distinguish the two people at one DNA marker.

{table-style=standard}
| Sample in this example | Marker found |
| --- | --- |
| Patient's skin sample before transplant | A |
| Donor's marrow before transplant | B |
| Patient's blood cells after transplant | B |

Did the patient's skin cells also switch to B? What tissue did the lab actually test?

@citation

Invented marker labels.  |  [MedlinePlus, bone marrow transplant](https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/003009.htm)

=== layout: subsection

# 18. Genetic Redesign of Animals

=== layout: bookend

# 18. Questions

@body

- How does a cross such as a mule differ from an animal with an engineered trait?
- Would people accept a hypothetical animal engineered only for food production, such as a chicken with extra legs? What arguments support or challenge this idea?
- What evidence would show an animal can function with a changed body plan?
- How do regulations differ for animals modified for food, for pets, and for research use?
- What about a chickapede, a chicken with 10 legs? That way more people can eat more chicken legs, but only kill one organism.

=== layout: one-panel

# 18. A Real Trait and a Thought Experiment

@body

- A mule is produced by crossing a horse and a donkey.
- AquAdvantage salmon was developed for a growth trait.
- The ten-legged chickapede is a thought experiment about changing a body plan.

@citation

[FDA, AquAdvantage Salmon fact sheet](https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/aquadvantage-salmon/aquadvantage-salmon-fact-sheet)

=== layout: one-panel

# 18. What Does the Chickapede Trade Off?

@body

- More drumsticks is one proposed benefit.
- Ask whether the animal can walk, feed, and behave normally.
- Which evidence would you want before deciding whether this is acceptable?

@citation

[NC3Rs, The three Rs](https://nc3rs.org.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/The%20Three%20Rs%20-%20The%20International%20Encyclopedia%20of%20Primatology%20-%20Prescott%20-%20John%20Wiley%20%26%20Sons%2C%20Inc..pdf)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![A Mule Is a Hybrid](assets/set7_instructor/mule_hybrid.jpg)

@caption

Topic 18: A Mule Is a Hybrid\
A mule inherits nuclear DNA from a horse and a donkey through a cross.

@citation

Course image: instructor ethics deck, p. 48.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![The Chickapede](assets/set7_instructor/chickapede.jpg)

@caption

Topic 18: The Chickapede\
An illustration for the thought experiment. What could extra legs cost the animal?

@citation

Course image: Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 50.

=== layout: one-panel

# 18. What Does Faster Growth Measure?

@body

The FDA described AquAdvantage Atlantic salmon as reaching a growth milestone more quickly than comparable farmed Atlantic salmon.

- Compare fish of the same age under comparable conditions.
- This evidence concerns the time needed to reach a size. Adult maximum size is a different measurement.

@citation

[FDA, AquAdvantage Salmon fact sheet](https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/aquadvantage-salmon/aquadvantage-salmon-fact-sheet)

=== layout: one-panel

# 18. Who Gets the Extra Drumsticks?

@body

Imagine the chickapede grows ten legs but struggles to reach food.

- A buyer gains meat per bird. The animal bears a cost every time it tries to eat.
- Choose a welfare observation that could change your verdict even if the yield claim were true.

@citation

[FDA, AquAdvantage Salmon fact sheet](https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/aquadvantage-salmon/aquadvantage-salmon-fact-sheet)

=== layout: one-panel

# 18. Three Questions Before an Animal Study

@body

- *Replacement:* Could a cell model answer this particular question?

- *Reduction:* How many animals are needed for an interpretable result?
- *Refinement:* How can the study reduce pain or distress?

Apply these to the chickapede. Which question about a whole animal's ability to walk would a cell model leave open?

@citation

[NC3Rs, The three Rs](https://nc3rs.org.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/The%20Three%20Rs%20-%20The%20International%20Encyclopedia%20of%20Primatology%20-%20Prescott%20-%20John%20Wiley%20%26%20Sons%2C%20Inc..pdf)

=== layout: two-panels

# 18. Faster Growth Is One Trait, Not a Welfare Verdict

@left

*Measure it*

- Age at harvest size
- Survival
- Feeding and swimming behavior

@right

*Then decide*

- Does the trait cause harm?
- Who benefits?
- What evidence changes the answer?

@citation

[FDA, AquAdvantage Salmon fact sheet](https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/aquadvantage-salmon/aquadvantage-salmon-fact-sheet)  |  [NC3Rs, The three Rs](https://nc3rs.org.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/The%20Three%20Rs%20-%20The%20International%20Encyclopedia%20of%20Primatology%20-%20Prescott%20-%20John%20Wiley%20%26%20Sons%2C%20Inc..pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 18. Turn the Chickapede Into a Testable Claim

@body

"Ten legs make more food per bird" is a yield claim. It does not answer whether an animal can use the legs, reach food, or avoid chronic injury.

- Pick an observable welfare outcome before building the argument.
- A cartoon is useful when it makes us name the evidence we would demand from a real proposal.

@citation

[NC3Rs, The three Rs](https://nc3rs.org.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/The%20Three%20Rs%20-%20The%20International%20Encyclopedia%20of%20Primatology%20-%20Prescott%20-%20John%20Wiley%20%26%20Sons%2C%20Inc..pdf)

=== layout: subsection

# 19. Consumer-Oriented or Trivial Transgenics

=== layout: bookend

# 19. Questions

@body

- Which transgenic animals are sold for their appearance? Use GloFish as one example.
- What does a regulator need to know before a fluorescent pet goes on sale?
- How did people respond to an artwork involving a live transgenic animal?
- Are there uses you would allow for health or agriculture but limit in art or entertainment? Why?
- What other traits might buyers want? Who would need to consider the animal's welfare?

=== layout: two-panels

# 19. The green part of the fish

@left

![Fluorescence overlaid on a photograph of a research zebrafish](assets/set7_photos/gfp_zebrafish.webp)

@right

Find the eye. Now follow the green signal toward the tail. The camera picked up light emitted by green fluorescent protein (GFP).

A biologist can use that signal as a marker. Why would a pet shop want a fluorescent fish?

@citation

[Photo: JTAVANTI, CC BY-SA 4.0](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fluorescent_Zebrafish_Juvenile.jpg)  |  [Chalfie et al., GFP as a marker (1994)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8303295/)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Where Does the Light Come From?](assets/set7_drawings/fluorescence.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 19: Where Does the Light Come From?\
GFP absorbs light and emits light at a longer wavelength.

@citation

[Chalfie et al., GFP as a marker for gene expression (1994)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8303295/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 19. Would the Purpose Change Your Decision?

@body

- Compare a fluorescent pet with a fluorescent laboratory animal.
- For each one, name the benefit, who receives it, and the welfare or environmental concern.
- Would your decision change if the fish could persist after escape?

@citation

[FDA, Risk-reviewed alterations in animals](https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/intentional-genomic-alterations-igas-animals/intentional-genomic-alterations-igas-animals-risk-reviewed-igas)

=== layout: two-panels

# 19. Alba and the Purpose of Bioart

@left

![Eduardo Kac, GFP Bunny artist](assets/set7_people_animals/eduardo_kac.jpg)

@right

Eduardo Kac presented a fluorescent rabbit named Alba as art. Compare the artist's purpose with the purpose of a fluorescent marker in research.

What responsibilities does an artist have when the work involves a living animal?

@citation

[Photo: SETI Institute, Eduardo Kac](https://www.seti.org/news/speculative-life-nominees/)  |  [Eduardo Kac, GFP Bunny (2000)](https://www.ekac.org/gfpbunny_essay.html)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Advertising a Fluorescent Trait](assets/set7_instructor/glofish_ad.jpg)

@caption

Topic 19: Advertising Fluorescence\
Which claims can this GloFish ad establish?

@citation

Course image: instructor ethics deck, p. 30.

=== layout: one-panel

# 19. Why Put a Fluorescent Protein in a Worm?

@body

Chalfie and colleagues used green fluorescent protein to show where a gene was expressed in living organisms, including a nematode.

- The fluorescent signal identified cells in which the marker was present.
- Put a similar trait in an aquarium fish and the buyer sees color. The observation can serve very different purposes.

@citation

[Chalfie et al., GFP as a marker for gene expression (1994)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8303295/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 19. Which Fish Did the Regulator Review?

@body

FDA lists specific lines of fluorescent fish in its inventory of risk-reviewed animal alterations.

- A finding about one line in one setting does not decide every question about every fluorescent pet.
- Suppose the climate at a possible release site changes. Which environmental assumption should be checked again?

@citation

[FDA, Risk-reviewed alterations in animals](https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/intentional-genomic-alterations-igas-animals/intentional-genomic-alterations-igas-animals-risk-reviewed-igas)

=== layout: two-panels

# 19. One Fluorescent Trait, Two Uses

@left

*Laboratory marker*

Signal can show where a gene is active in a living animal.

@right

*Consumer fish*

Signal changes visible appearance for a buyer.

*Discussion*

The molecule is similar. Do purpose and downstream effects change your ethical judgment?

@citation

[Chalfie et al., GFP as a marker for gene expression (1994)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8303295/)  |  [FDA, Risk-reviewed alterations in animals](https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/intentional-genomic-alterations-igas-animals/intentional-genomic-alterations-igas)

=== layout: two-panels

# 19. Why photograph an ordinary fish too?

@left

*Fish with the fluorescent protein*

You see a bright green patch under the excitation light. Before you say where the protein is, check what else gives off light.

@right

*Fish without the fluorescent protein*

Look at the same part of this fish under the same light and camera settings. If both fish glow there, what else could explain your picture?

@citation

Class comparison adapted from [Chalfie et al., GFP as a marker for gene expression (1994)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8303295/)

=== layout: subsection

# 20. Kevin Esvelt and Lyme Disease

=== layout: bookend

# 20. Questions

@body

- How has Esvelt engaged the Nantucket and Martha's Vineyard communities in the decision-making process?
- What evidence would support a reduction in Lyme disease risk?

=== layout: two-panels

# 20. Mice, Ticks, and People

@left

![Kevin Esvelt, Mice Against Ticks researcher](assets/set7_people_animals/kevin_esvelt.jpg)

@right

Kevin Esvelt discussed a proposal to interrupt Lyme transmission through white-footed mice. Ticks can acquire the bacteria from mice and later transmit them to people.

The Mice Against Ticks proposal sought community input before an outdoor trial.

@citation

[Photo: Brookhaven National Laboratory, Kevin Esvelt](https://www.bnl.gov/newsroom/news.php?a=112257)  |  [CDC, How Lyme disease spreads](https://www.cdc.gov/lyme/causes/index.html)  |  [Buchthal et al., Mice Against Ticks (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30905296/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 20. What Would Demonstrate a Benefit?

@body

- Count infected ticks as an intermediate measure. A change in human infections is a different outcome.
- Ask about comparison sites and other changes over time.
- Local residents should have a say in whether research proceeds in their community.

@citation

[Buchthal et al., Mice Against Ticks (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30905296/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 20. Separate the Proposal from a General Gene-drive Debate

@body

- People in Nantucket and Martha's Vineyard discussed whether a local intervention was acceptable.
- The Mice Against Ticks proposal and proposals for self-spreading interventions raise distinct oversight questions.
- Keep community consent, research results, and proof of public-health benefit separate.

@citation

[Buchthal et al., Mice Against Ticks (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30905296/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 20. What Did Residents Help Decide?

@body

The Mice Against Ticks authors described meetings with residents of Nantucket and Martha's Vineyard before a possible field study.

- Residents raised preferences about what kinds of changes would be acceptable.
- A meeting can influence the research question. Attendance alone cannot stand in for community agreement.

@citation

[Buchthal et al., Mice Against Ticks (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30905296/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 20. Fewer Infected Ticks Per Walk?

@body

Suppose 20 of 100 ticks at a site carried the bacteria last year. This year 15 of 150 carry them.

- The fraction fell from 20% to 10%. The number collected also changed.
- To compare exposure on a walk, ask how much area and time each survey covered. Human case counts answer a further question.

@citation

[CDC, How Lyme disease spreads](https://www.cdc.gov/lyme/causes/index.html)  |  [Buchthal et al., Mice Against Ticks (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30905296/)
=== layout: big-image

@image

![Local Lyme Outcome Ladder](assets/set7_animals_evidence/outcomes_ladder.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 20: What Outcome Answers the Question?\
Tick infection, exposure, and disease cases answer different questions.

@citation

[CDC, How Lyme disease spreads](https://www.cdc.gov/lyme/causes/index.html)  |  [Buchthal et al., Mice Against Ticks (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30905296/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 20. A Community Can Change the Research Question

@body

The Mice Against Ticks team reported meetings with residents before a potential field study on Nantucket and Martha's Vineyard.

- Community discussion can identify which outcomes and tradeoffs matter locally.
- A public meeting documents input. It does not automatically establish agreement across everyone affected.

@citation

[Buchthal et al., Mice Against Ticks (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30905296/)
